### F5 — MEDIUM: one certificate file makes *every* name on the site "secured"

**`/opt/nxeon/worker/migrate-real.ts:3512-3518`**, with `certExists` at **`:3268-3271`**

```ts
for (const n of site.names) {
  if (await certExists(conn, n)) {
    secured.push(...site.names);            // ← ALL names, from ONE match
    await say(`  ${n}: HTTPS already covered by the certificate migrated from the old server.`);

// :3268
async function certExists(conn, domain) {
  return shOk(conn, `test -s /etc/letsencrypt/live/${domain}/fullchain.pem && test -s .../privkey.pem`);
}
```

`certExists` is a bare file-existence test: no expiry check, no SAN inspection. If a site has names `[a.com, b.com, c.com]` and only `a.com` has a `live/` directory, all three land in `secured`. Then `runRealMigration` pushes `HTTPS for 3 hostname(s)` with **`status: "running"`** and detail *"a valid certificate is in place for a.com, b.com, c.com, and automatic renewal is configured"*. A migrated-but-long-expired cert also passes.

- **Customer sees:** a green, verified line saying HTTPS is done for names it is not done for.
- **What is true:** `b.com`/`c.com` present the wrong certificate — the 2026-07-25 `studionexis.com` failure mode.
- **Partial mitigation (why this is MEDIUM, not HIGH):** the end-of-run probe at `:5643-5651` runs `servedCertNames` + `certCoversName` per domain and *does* flip `serving:false`, so the **verdict** is usually still correct. It is the step line that lies.

**Smallest fix:** push per-name, gated on coverage and expiry:
```ts
const names = await servedCertNames(conn, n);   // already exists, :2329
for (const m of site.names) if (names && certCoversName(names, m)) secured.push(m);
```
plus `openssl x509 -checkend 0 -noout -in /etc/letsencrypt/live/<n>/fullchain.pem`.

